From: Jordan Bettcher <thebettcher@gmail.com>
Subject: v8: ppc64 detect Maglev Int32 multiply overflow in CR0, not XER

Maglev's Int32MultiplyWithOverflow is the last Int32 overflow site in the
ppc64 backend still reading XER.  Turbofan's kPPC_Mul32 was moved to an
extsw+cmpd compare by ppc64le-v8-mul32-neg32-overflow-cmpd.patch; this
applies the same identity in Maglev, which sees the same arithmetic from
the same JS.

The withdrawn ppc64le-v8-mul32-overflow-synth.patch carried a version of
this hunk that tested the product with mulhw/mullw/srawi/cmpw -- four
instructions and a second scratch register.  That is unnecessary: mullw
reads only the low word of each operand and places the full 64-bit product
in RT (Power ISA, Book I, "Multiply Low Word"), so the int32 product
overflows exactly when it is not its own sign extension, and

    mullw temp,left,right
    extsw out,temp
    cmpd  out,temp

decides it in three instructions with the one scratch register the function
already holds.  The extsw is not wasted work either -- ZeroExtWord32 below
re-narrows the same low word, so the result is bit-identical to what mullw
alone produced.  The patch that hunk came from was withdrawn for its
Turbofan Add32/Sub32 hunks, which assumed Word32 values arrive
sign-extended; nothing here rests on that, because mullw ignores the
operands' upper words entirely.

Ordering: temp takes the untruncated product, so out is written only after
both inputs have been read, and out may alias left or right.  temp is the
existing TemporaryRegisterScope scratch and is reused further down for the
-0 check, after this sequence has finished with it.  DCHECK_REGLIST_EMPTY
keeps the same register list.

Polarity: EmitEagerDeoptIf moves from overflow32 to ne, matching the CR0
compare.  The other EmitEagerDeoptIf(overflow32, ...) sites in this file
are untouched -- they belong to add/subtract/negate/divide, which this
identity does not cover.

Why it matters on POWER8: mcrxr has been emulated rather than implemented
since POWER4, and the loadpc synthesis this backend uses in its place
measured 2805 ns/op against 4.58 ns for the sequence above.  On POWER9 the
native mcrxrx measures 4.61 ns, i.e. parity, so this is a large win on one
pool and free on the other.

ISA: mullw, extsw and cmpd are all ISA 2.07 or older, so this is valid for
both pools.

NOT YET EXERCISED.  This was written against the extracted 153.0.8010.52
tree and applies cleanly after the eleven unconditional ppc64le-v8-*
patches, but no binary has been built from it and no benchmark has been
run.  The mullw full-product identity it rests on is hardware-verified on
POWER9 (196 operand pairs against __builtin_mul_overflow, including
garbage upper words) by the Turbofan patch above; what is unverified here
is this function, not the identity.  Upstreamable as-is.

--- a/v8/src/maglev/ppc/maglev-ir-ppc.cc
+++ b/v8/src/maglev/ppc/maglev-ir-ppc.cc
@@ -422,17 +422,24 @@
 
   MaglevAssembler::TemporaryRegisterScope temps(masm);
   Register temp = temps.AcquireScratch();
-  // Read XER[OV32] via mcrxrx, not CR0[SO]. SetRC copies the *sticky*
-  // XER[SO] into CR0 bit 3, and nothing in a syscall-free stretch of JS ever
-  // clears it -- so after a single genuine overflow every later int32 multiply
-  // would deopt forever, permanently dropping the function out of Maglev.
-  // This matches the idiom every other overflow site in this file uses.
-  __ mullw(out, left, right, SetOE);
-  __ MoveToCrFromXer(cr0);
+  // mullw reads only the low word of each operand and leaves the whole 64-bit
+  // product in its target, so the int32 product overflows exactly when it is
+  // not its own sign extension -- which cmpd reports in CR0 without reading
+  // XER at all. Two other options were rejected: SetRC copies the *sticky*
+  // XER[SO] into CR0 bit 3, which nothing in a syscall-free stretch of JS ever
+  // clears, so one genuine overflow would deopt every later int32 multiply
+  // forever and drop the function out of Maglev permanently; and reading
+  // XER[OV32] needs mcrxrx or, on POWER8, the emulated mcrxr, measured at
+  // 2805 ns against 4.58 ns for this sequence, with POWER9's native mcrxrx at
+  // parity (4.61 ns). `temp` takes the untruncated product, so `out` is not
+  // written until both inputs have been read and may alias either of them.
+  __ mullw(temp, left, right);
+  __ extsw(out, temp);
+  __ CmpS64(out, temp, cr0);
 
   DCHECK_REGLIST_EMPTY(RegList{temp, out} &
                        GetGeneralRegistersUsedAsInputs(eager_deopt_info()));
-  __ EmitEagerDeoptIf(overflow32, DeoptimizeReason::kOverflow, this);
+  __ EmitEagerDeoptIf(ne, DeoptimizeReason::kOverflow, this);
 
   // Making sure that the 32-bit output is zero-extended.
   __ ZeroExtWord32(out, out);
